<h1>Strata Pricing: Complete 2026 Breakdown</h1>

<p>Strata, operated by FreeWheel, is an enterprise media planning, buying, billing, and reconciliation platform used by agencies that manage cross-channel media. It is not sold as a self-serve SaaS tool with public monthly plans.</p>

<p>Most buyers should expect a custom annual quote. Scope usually depends on agency size, markets, enabled modules, financial integrations, and whether digital buying workflows are included.</p>

<h2>Pricing Model</h2>

<p>Strata uses a <strong>custom enterprise subscription model</strong>. The contract is typically shaped around the number of users, client or office footprint, media channels supported, and workflow modules such as IO management, billing, reconciliation, and reporting.</p>

<p>Because Strata is tied to agency operations and finance workflows, implementation effort matters as much as the software license. Accounting integrations, campaign taxonomy, permissions, and billing processes can all affect total cost.</p>

<h2>Pricing Tiers</h2>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Tier</th><th>Pricing</th><th>Best For</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Core Media Management</td><td>Custom quote</td><td>Agencies needing planning, buying, IO, and campaign workflow tools</td></tr>
<tr><td>Expanded Financial Workflows</td><td>Custom quote</td><td>Teams adding billing, reconciliation, and accounting integrations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Enterprise / Network</td><td>Custom quote</td><td>Large agencies or multi-office organizations standardizing media operations</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Public software directories such as <a href="https://www.g2.com/products/freewheel-strata/reviews">G2</a> list Strata reviews but do not show standard public pricing. Use those reviews for workflow context, not as a quote source.</p>

<h2>What's Included</h2>

<p>Strata contracts can include planning, activation, IO management, campaign tracking, billing, invoicing, reconciliation, and reporting. Exact module access depends on the negotiated package.</p>

<p>Agency buyers should clarify whether digital campaign workflows, FreeWheel DSP connectivity, accounting bridges, custom reports, and data exports are included or priced separately.</p>

<h2>Total Cost of Ownership</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Implementation:</strong> Migration from spreadsheets or legacy buying systems can require taxonomy cleanup, workflow design, and finance stakeholder involvement.</li>
<li><strong>Financial integrations:</strong> Accounting system connections are often the most important source of implementation complexity.</li>
<li><strong>Training:</strong> Planners, buyers, finance users, and operations leads may need different onboarding paths.</li>
<li><strong>Data migration:</strong> Historical campaigns, vendors, client codes, and rate structures can add services work.</li>
<li><strong>Ongoing administration:</strong> Enterprise media systems require role management, vendor maintenance, and process governance.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Who It's Best For / Not For</h2>

<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Agencies with meaningful linear, digital, and cross-media buying volume that need one operational system for buying, IOs, billing, and reconciliation.</p>

<p><strong>Not ideal for:</strong> Small teams looking only for a lightweight planning calendar or media plan presentation tool. If you do not need financial reconciliation or operational buying workflows, tools like <a href="/bionic">Bionic</a>, <a href="/bluhorn">BluHorn</a>, or <a href="/camphouse">Camphouse</a> may be easier to evaluate.</p>

